    "evidence_text": "Cognitive behavioral therapy vs zopiclone for treatment of chronic primary insomnia in older adults: a randomized controlled trial.\n\nFor most outcomes, zopiclone did not differ from placebo.\n\nThe study used a pre-post intervention design.   \n\n\nCONCLUSION A brief cognitive behavioral intervention can reduce binge eating behaviors among bariatric surgery candidates.\n\nBehavioral treatment of obesity in patients encountered in primary care settings: a systematic review.\n\nNo studies were found in which primary care practitioners delivered counseling that followed the CMS guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANCE Sleep disturbances are most prevalent among older adults and often go untreated.\n\nTreatment options for sleep disturbances remain limited, and there is a need for community-accessible programs that can improve sleep.   \n\n\nSleep is modified by various cytokines including IL-6.\n\nA mental health intervention for schoolchildren exposed to violence: a randomized controlled trial.\n\nNeither treatment, however, eliminated headache problems.\n\nTreatments for somnambulism in adults: assessing the evidence.\n\nClinicians will need to assess the evidence for treatment options.\n\nNo properly powered rigorous controlled trials were found for treatment of sleepwalking in adults.\n\nA clinical approach to circadian rhythm sleep disorders.\n\nDiagnostic tools such as sleep diaries and wrist activity monitoring are often useful in confirming the diagnosis.",
